<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.5: Identifying Graphs of Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01955</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.5: Identifying Graphs of Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b037e657-a260-4ca8-8258-f77df075fcac</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Here are graphs that represent three quadratic functions, defined by:</para>
      <para>\(f(x)=x^2-4\)
  \(g(x)=1-x^2\)
  \(h(x)=x^2+4\)</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Coordinate plane with 3 quadratic functions. Curve A opens up and has minimum at 0 comma 4. Curve B opens up and has minimum at negative 4 comma 0. Curve C opens down and has maximum 0 comma 1.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_12_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_200d8791-5375-4d74-8a94-8d7d0b9bad61" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x^2-4\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Blue Graph B because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-4)\) and the graph opens upward.</para>
            <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Blue Graph B because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-4)\) and the graph opens upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cd497fb3-7b75-4099-a3c2-2278ada45ded" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(g(x)=1-x^2\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Black Graph C because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,1)\) and the graph opens downward.</para>
            <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Black Graph C because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,1)\) and the graph opens downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5007f1e3-3da1-4798-a4ba-f8447ef38153" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph represents the equation \(h(x)=x^2+4\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Green Graph A because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,4)\) and the graph opens upward.</para>
            <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Green Graph A because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,4)\) and the graph opens upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset-problem" data-problem-comparator="text" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-x^2">
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-problem-content">
    <ol class="os-raise-noindent">
      <li>Which part of the equation tells us that Graph C opens downward?</li>
    </ol>
  
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-correct-response">
    <p>Correct! The negative coefficient of \(x^2\) makes the graph open downward.</p>
  
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-attempts-exhausted-response">

  

-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which part of the equation tells us that Graph C opens downward?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(y=1-x^2\) is the equation. The negative coefficient of \(x^2\) makes the graph open downward.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content></document>
